   528   * \brief Write the graph to a stream in GML format
   529   *
   530   * GML is a quite general textual format, see
   531   * http://www.fim.uni-passau.de/en/fim/faculty/chairs/theoretische-informatik/projects.html for details.
   532   *
   533   * </para><para> The graph, vertex and edges attributes are written to the
   534   * file as well, if they are numeric or string.
   535   *
   536   * </para><para> As igraph is more forgiving about attribute names, it might
   537   * be necessary to simplify the them before writing to the GML file.
   538   * This way we'll have a syntactically correct GML file. The following
   539   * simple procedure is performed on each attribute name: first the alphanumeric
   540   * characters are extracted, the others are ignored. Then if the first character
   541   * is not a letter then the attribute name is prefixed with <quote>igraph</quote>.
   542   * Note that this might result identical names for two attributes, igraph
   543   * does not check this.
   544   *
   545   * </para><para> The <quote>id</quote> vertex attribute is treated specially.
   546   * If the <parameter>id</parameter> argument is not 0 then it should be a numeric
   547   * vector with the vertex ids and the <quote>id</quote> vertex attribute is
   548   * ignored (if there is one). If <parameter>id</parameter> is 0 and there is a
   549   * numeric <quote>id</quote> vertex attribute that is used instead. If ids
   550   * are not specified in either way then the regular igraph vertex ids are used.
   551   *
   552   * </para><para> Note that whichever way vertex ids are specified, their
   553   * uniqueness is not checked.
   554   *
   555   * </para><para> If the graph has edge attributes named <quote>source</quote>
   556   * or <quote>target</quote> they're silently ignored. GML uses these attributes
